course: dictionary
lesson: 3229
title: Entire: the whole thing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word entire means whole, with every part included. If Ruby built an entire castle, then the whole castle was built, with every tower and wall finished.
explain: «In Minecraft, Ruby built an entire castle with towers while Theo helped by digging holes, one of which Ruby fell into.» Swap in whole: Ruby built a whole castle with towers. It still makes sense, so whole is a good meaning for entire here.
explain: Entire can also mean complete or total, as in «with his entire attention». Here it means all of his attention, not just some of it. Ask yourself: is the whole thing included, or only a part?
fact: Entire means whole, with every part included.
fact: «Entire» can also mean complete or total.
fact: Entirely is the adverb form, so it describes how something is done, as in «entirely finished».
fact: Useful phrases: «the entire world», «the entire time», and «in its entirety», which means as a whole.
fact: In maths, a fraction is a part of an entire thing, so the entire amount is the whole total.
exercise: «I ate the entire pizza.» What does entire mean here? | Cold and soggy | Burnt and black | *All of it, the whole pizza | Only a slice
exercise: In maths, what is the entire amount? | *The whole total | A small part of the total | The very last digit | A guess
exercise: Which word is the adverb form of entire? | entry | *entirely | entire | enter
test: Ruby built an entire castle. What does entire tell you? | *The whole castle was built, with every part included | The castle fell down | Only part of a castle was built | A small wall was built
test: Which phrase means the whole world? | *the entire world | one room in the house | a tiny corner of the map | the nearest shop
exam: Which sentence uses entire to mean complete or total? | *She gave his story her entire attention | The rain stopped | We walked to the park | The dog barked once
